WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.885
Think of a group task
you have helped organize:

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:07.590
a family gathering,
a volunteer activity, or a meeting.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:13.510
Confusion often begins when people
assume someone else knows what to do.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:15.790
We will coordinate a fictional

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:19.270
community computer-help
session using a clear message,

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:20.970
one shared handout,

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:26.430
and an agreed review process.
You can connect the example to a group

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.250
you know, without entering
real members' information.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.850
The goal is not to send more messages.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:37.430
It is to help the right person
take the right next action.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.210
By the end,

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.631
you should be able to choose a channel,

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:46.370
request useful feedback, and verify
the decision everyone will follow.

00:00:48.138 --> 00:00:51.798
A live conversation can help
resolve an urgent misunderstanding,

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:56.256
while a written message can preserve
details for someone who responds later.

00:00:57.198 --> 00:01:01.239
A shared document is useful when several
people must work on the same content.

00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:04.978
Choose the channel by the task,
timing, and audience.

00:01:05.938 --> 00:01:09.047
Check which account you are
using before posting or sending.

00:01:09.618 --> 00:01:13.858
A personal account and an organization
account may have different audiences,

00:01:14.258 --> 00:01:16.158
permissions, and expectations.

00:01:17.338 --> 00:01:22.178
Do not assume that a private-looking group
makes every detail appropriate to share.

00:01:23.638 --> 00:01:24.598
Pause and choose

00:01:24.598 --> 00:01:25.658
a channel for a partner

00:01:25.658 --> 00:01:28.819
who cannot meet today but needs
to review tomorrow's handout.

00:01:29.458 --> 00:01:30.478
A clear written

00:01:30.478 --> 00:01:34.298
request with the shared document
gives them a useful starting point.

00:01:36.244 --> 00:01:39.644
Our subject line is Review
the computer-help handout.

00:01:40.064 --> 00:01:42.499
The message says which
section needs attention,

00:01:43.144 --> 00:01:46.460
what kind of feedback would help,
and when a response is useful.

00:01:46.944 --> 00:01:48.324
For example: please

00:01:48.324 --> 00:01:50.464
comment on whether the contact section is

00:01:50.464 --> 00:01:52.584
clear before our next practice session.

00:01:53.264 --> 00:01:55.803
That is more actionable
than please look at this.

00:01:56.464 --> 00:01:57.214
Use respectful,

00:01:57.584 --> 00:02:01.424
direct language and explain
abbreviations the reader may not know.

00:02:01.944 --> 00:02:05.984
Attach or link the intended file,
then check that it is the correct version.

00:02:06.624 --> 00:02:09.577
Read the message once from
the recipient's point of view.

00:02:09.904 --> 00:02:12.236
Could they act without
asking what you meant?

00:02:12.444 --> 00:02:13.644
In this course, draft

00:02:13.644 --> 00:02:17.184
the message with fictional information;
you do not need to send it.

00:02:18.755 --> 00:02:22.595 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Before sending a real email,
inspect every recipient field.

00:02:22.595 --> 00:02:25.095 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
To usually identifies the main recipients.

00:02:26.155 --> 00:02:29.195 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Cc sends a visible copy to
people who need to be informed.

00:02:29.975 --> 00:02:33.455 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Bcc hides those recipients'
addresses from the other recipients,

00:02:34.235 --> 00:02:35.175 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
but it does not make

00:02:35.175 --> 00:02:38.375 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
the message confidential or
stop someone from forwarding it.

00:02:38.875 --> 00:02:42.295 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Reply all can send your response
to more people than you intended.

00:02:42.615 --> 00:02:44.843 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Check whether everyone
needs that response.

00:02:45.555 --> 00:02:46.655 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Timing also matters:

00:02:46.655 --> 00:02:51.375 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
a late message with an immediate deadline
may be difficult for others to act on.

00:02:51.795 --> 00:02:53.764 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause and review your fictional draft.

00:02:54.475 --> 00:02:58.620 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Remove unnecessary recipients and give
the partner a reasonable response window.

00:02:59.035 --> 00:03:02.655 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Clear communication includes both
the content and its destination.

00:03:04.702 --> 00:03:05.682
Imagine three

00:03:05.682 --> 00:03:10.122
people editing three separate
handouts and calling each one final.

00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:13.832
It becomes difficult to know
which instructions to use.

00:03:14.262 --> 00:03:18.922
Instead, agree where the common copy lives
and what each person is responsible for.

00:03:19.442 --> 00:03:21.566
The owner coordinates the final decision,

00:03:21.822 --> 00:03:24.827
the writer drafts,
and the reviewer suggests improvements.

00:03:25.642 --> 00:03:28.202
Give each person access
appropriate to that task,

00:03:28.202 --> 00:03:30.762
and verify that the link opens for them.

00:03:31.262 --> 00:03:34.526
Working together at the same time
is synchronous collaboration.

00:03:35.242 --> 00:03:38.195
Leaving comments for later is
asynchronous collaboration.

00:03:38.962 --> 00:03:40.620
Neither is automatically better.

00:03:41.002 --> 00:03:44.940
Choose the arrangement that fits people's
schedules and the decision needed,

00:03:45.042 --> 00:03:47.922
then record the agreed
outcome in the shared place.

00:03:49.836 --> 00:03:51.056 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Compare two comments.

00:03:51.436 --> 00:03:54.176 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
This is confusing gives the
writer little to act on.

00:03:54.416 --> 00:03:55.496 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Add the contact number

00:03:55.496 --> 00:03:59.236 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
after the steps so readers can
find help identifies a location,

00:03:59.596 --> 00:04:00.916 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
a change, and a reason.

00:04:01.236 --> 00:04:02.691 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The writer can accept the idea,

00:04:02.896 --> 00:04:05.776 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
ask a clarifying question,
or explain another choice.

00:04:06.316 --> 00:04:08.803 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Discuss the work rather
than judging the person.

00:04:09.076 --> 00:04:13.169 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
After making the edit, acknowledge the
feedback and check the revised document.

00:04:13.336 --> 00:04:14.436 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Resolve a comment only

00:04:14.436 --> 00:04:18.276 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
when the issue has been addressed
or an alternative decision is clear.

00:04:18.636 --> 00:04:21.916 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause and write one useful
comment on the fictional handout.

00:04:22.256 --> 00:04:23.196 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Have a partner explain

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:23.956 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
the change they

00:04:23.956 --> 00:04:26.288 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
think you are requesting
before they make it.

00:04:27.865 --> 00:04:31.958
Before a practice meeting, select and
test the intended speaker and microphone.

00:04:32.665 --> 00:04:34.205
Check the app's controls rather

00:04:34.205 --> 00:04:37.677
than assuming the computer's general
settings tell the whole story.

00:04:38.105 --> 00:04:42.785
Mute when appropriate, and use the agreed
chat or raise-hand control to contribute.

00:04:43.585 --> 00:04:44.305
A webinar may give

00:04:44.305 --> 00:04:47.777
the host more control over who can
speak than a small meeting does.

00:04:48.305 --> 00:04:51.345
Follow the event's instructions
and ask before recording.

00:04:52.045 --> 00:04:55.565
If captions are available and helpful,
turn them on; ask

00:04:55.565 --> 00:04:57.905
for clarification when
a term is unfamiliar.

00:04:59.025 --> 00:05:00.152
If the connection fails,

00:05:00.445 --> 00:05:04.285
use the agreed backup plan, such as
reading the shared notes afterward.

00:05:04.885 --> 00:05:08.875
Preparation reduces the amount of meeting
time spent guessing about controls.

00:05:11.003 --> 00:05:15.183
A community page can be useful for
finding an event or asking a question,

00:05:15.663 --> 00:05:19.323
but members' posts are not automatically
verified service information.

00:05:19.683 --> 00:05:21.704
Read the community's purpose and rules,

00:05:22.003 --> 00:05:25.164
observe how people ask for help,
and contribute respectfully.

00:05:25.703 --> 00:05:26.454
For eligibility,

00:05:26.623 --> 00:05:27.373
opening hours,

00:05:27.803 --> 00:05:28.977
or official requirements,

00:05:29.243 --> 00:05:33.803
check the responsible organization through
its known website or contact method.

00:05:34.243 --> 00:05:36.626
Keep private details out
of a public question.

00:05:37.083 --> 00:05:39.323
If someone suggests an unfamiliar service,

00:05:39.743 --> 00:05:42.203
record what needs
confirmation before acting.

00:05:42.743 --> 00:05:45.696
You already use judgment when
accepting advice in person.

00:05:45.903 --> 00:05:48.023
Bring that same habit online: consider

00:05:48.023 --> 00:05:49.243
who is speaking, what

00:05:49.243 --> 00:05:52.559
they can know, and where the claim
can be checked independently.

00:05:54.141 --> 00:05:58.381
A fictional offer says free trial today,
then twelve dollars each month.

00:05:58.921 --> 00:06:01.563
Before accepting,
identify what is being purchased:

00:06:02.041 --> 00:06:05.501
a physical good, a one-time service,
or a continuing subscription.

00:06:06.341 --> 00:06:07.561
Check the amount due now,

00:06:08.041 --> 00:06:08.791
future charges,

00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:13.001
renewal timing, cancellation conditions,
and the seller's identity.

00:06:13.641 --> 00:06:16.441
A small in-app purchase
can still cost real money,

00:06:16.861 --> 00:06:18.981
and several small purchases can add up.

00:06:19.321 --> 00:06:23.781
A familiar logo or a professional page is
not proof that every offer is suitable.

00:06:23.781 --> 00:06:26.481
Our practice ends with
explaining the decision;

00:06:27.101 --> 00:06:29.381
no purchase or payment
details are required.

00:06:30.101 --> 00:06:30.861
Pause and name

00:06:30.861 --> 00:06:35.006
the fact you would need if the future
price or cancellation method were missing.

00:06:36.652 --> 00:06:39.554
Complete the fictional
coordination task with a partner.

00:06:39.892 --> 00:06:40.737
Draft the request,

00:06:41.192 --> 00:06:43.232
choose the recipients and access role,

00:06:43.552 --> 00:06:48.412
propose a useful comment, and record
the final decision in one shared place.

00:06:48.832 --> 00:06:51.292
Switch roles so both
people practice explaining

00:06:51.292 --> 00:06:55.152
and checking. Then review the
subscription example and state what

00:06:55.152 --> 00:06:57.276
you would verify before committing money.

00:06:57.672 --> 00:07:00.172
Judge success by observable results:

00:07:00.952 --> 00:07:02.314
the partner knows what to do,

00:07:02.552 --> 00:07:05.732
can open the right file,
and understands the agreed outcome.

00:07:06.472 --> 00:07:09.892
For a group task from your own life,
choose one habit to transfer.

00:07:10.452 --> 00:07:11.860
It might be a clearer subject,

00:07:12.112 --> 00:07:12.910
fewer recipients,

00:07:13.592 --> 00:07:18.252
one common copy, or a written decision
that prevents the next round of confusion.
